<!--
    RE-friction round 9, need `hint-printing-call-loses` (challenge
    5e51836e33c5d4439bb2dc24, ELF x86-64 `cr4ckm3_0x`): the function at 0x400f30
    decodes a 23-byte string with an unrolled XOR loop and hands the buffer to
    its hint printer, and `kuna decompile` renders that call with no arguments,

        sub_417f80();                         0x401007

    although 0x400f58 `lea rdi,[rbp-0x30]` sets rdi to the buffer and nothing
    redefines rdi before the CALL.  Asserting a prototype for the callee restores
    `sub_417f80((char *)v2)`, so the caller-side dataflow is intact and the loss
    is entirely in unlocked call-site recovery.

    Root cause (P4, trial finalization).  `calleearitybody` picks the run {rdi} -
    the callee reads rdi at 0x417f84 - and its decode is cut three instructions
    later at 0x417f8d, so nothing proves rsi dead and `calleearitycut` is asked
    to bound the run by the register it stops at.  `calleearitycut` requires that
    register to be QUIET, meaning the caller put no value in it, and the caller
    did: 0x400fc9 `movzx esi,byte ptr [rbp-0x1a]` puts the XOR key in rsi and
    0x400fcd `xor byte ptr [r8+0x6],sil` consumes it.  The boundary is not quiet,
    the run is refused, and the whole one-argument list is dropped.

    But "the caller wrote it" and "the caller is passing it" are different
    claims, and a register allocator will spend an argument register on a
    throwaway.  Argument setup is a value used for NOTHING but the call, which is
    `Funcdata::onlyOpUse` (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) - and upstream has already
    asked it: `FuncCallSpecs::checkInputTrialUse` runs `ancestorOpUse` over every
    trial and marks it ACTIVE only when the answer is yes.  The XOR is the other
    use, so rsi's trial scores INACTIVE, and an inactive boundary is scratch.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "trial-finalization".  The P0 assertion
    `option calleearityscratch on|off` decides whether an inactive boundary can
    end the run; it is inert with `calleearitycut off`.

    The fixture is the same construct on SysV x86-64.  Every callee reads rdi and
    then calls `sink`, so the callee-body walk is cut there and the run {rdi} has
    no proven boundary; every caller passes its own incoming rdi straight through
    (`AncestorRealistic::execute` refuses an input Varnode, so the site recovers
    nothing) and differs only in what it puts in rsi:

        sink:  ret
        calN:  mov %rdi,%rax; call sink; ret

        scratch_wide:   push %rbx; mov %rdi,%rbx
                        mov (%rdi),%rsi ; add %rsi,%rbx   # caller consumes rsi
                        call cal1 ; add %ebx,%eax ; pop %rbx ; ret
        scratch_byte:   ... movzbl (%rdi),%esi ; xor %sil,0x4(%rdi) ...
        boundary_live:  ... mov (%rdi),%rsi ...            # nothing else uses it
        boundary_const: ... mov $2,%esi ; add %rsi,%rbx ...

    `scratch_wide` and `scratch_byte` are the fix.  The other two are the
    discriminators, unchanged in BOTH passes: `boundary_live` uses rsi for
    nothing but the call, so its trial scores active and the site recovers rsi on
    its own, and `boundary_const` materializes a constant, which is argument
    setup in its most literal form and is never scratch however the trial scored.
    Pass 1 (`option calleearityscratch off`) reproduces the BUG in the first two.
-->
